From d2d0d545174235920c45b1ac8781a02906883ef6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 13:48:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] A drain of the decoder keeps the picture
 the renderer had no room for

The video thread drains the decoder for its last pictures at the end of a
stream (VIDEO_DRAIN), before another stream is opened (GENERAL_STREAMCHANGE)
and when the stream stalls on a still picture: it asked the decoder for a
picture and offered it to the renderer until the decoder had none left.
When the renderer has no free buffer within the wait of OutputPicture(),
ProcessDecoderOutput() returns with the picture still in m_picture
(OUTPUT_AGAIN); the loop asked the decoder for the next picture all the
same, and CDVDVideoCodec::GetPicture() releases the picture it is given to
fill, so the waiting picture was let go unseen. Every picture after it then
waited for a buffer in turn, up to 500 ms each, while the thread read no
message: paused near the end of a file, or with the output stalled, the last
pictures were lost and the player did not answer for that long. This is
Kodi's own code; the fault was found in CroqueMr's build (CroqueMr/xbmc
6096ec07c0), which solves it another way.

Now the three drains go through DrainDecoder()
(KODI::VIDEOPLAYER::DECODER_DRAIN, DecoderDrain.h): a picture that waits is
offered again while output can move (not paused, nothing called off), up to
three more times, and the decoder is asked for the next one only once it has
gone out. If it still waits, the drain stops with the picture kept: at the
end of a stream and on a still picture the loop of the thread offers it
again as it does any picture that waits, answering messages meanwhile, and
drains the rest after it (the decoder keeps its drain control until the next
packet). Before another stream is opened the old decoder is replaced, so
there a picture that still waits is let go with what the decoder still holds,
as before, but without each of them waiting in turn (a debug line says so).
Playback that never fills the renderer is unchanged.
